WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.301
Tool calling and function calling let an agent request capabilities from Python code or connected services.

2
00:00:07.301 --> 00:00:19.918
A tool may search documents, send email, create tickets, update calendars, query databases, read files, call APIs, inspect code, browse approved resources, or operate workflow systems.

3
00:00:19.918 --> 00:00:23.648
Tools are where model output can turn into real effects.

4
00:00:23.648 --> 00:00:28.886
That makes the tool layer one of the most important places to enforce security.

5
00:00:28.886 --> 00:00:32.854
A secure tool design starts with allowlists and structure.

6
00:00:32.854 --> 00:00:42.773
The application should define which tools exist, which users or agent modes may use them, what actions are allowed, and what arguments are expected.

7
00:00:42.773 --> 00:00:46.106
Structured arguments make validation possible.

8
00:00:46.106 --> 00:00:56.184
Before execution, the tool wrapper should validate identifiers, tenant context, destination, size, type, format, operation, and business intent.

9
00:00:56.184 --> 00:01:04.041
The model should not be able to invent new tools or pass arbitrary free-form instructions to sensitive capabilities.

10
00:01:04.041 --> 00:01:08.802
Read-only tools and write-capable tools require different controls.

11
00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:20.626
Read-only tools can still leak data, but write-capable tools can change systems, send messages, modify records, trigger workflows, create cost, or affect external services.

12
00:01:20.626 --> 00:01:31.101
High-impact tools should use scoped credentials, policy checks, approval gates, dry-run or preview modes, rate limits, quotas, and durable audit trails.

13
00:01:31.101 --> 00:01:36.100
The user should understand what action is about to occur before it happens.

14
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.148
Model output should not directly become a sensitive action.

15
00:01:40.148 --> 00:01:51.099
The application should check authorization, validate tool arguments, enforce policy, record the decision, and require human approval where impact warrants it.

16
00:01:51.099 --> 00:01:59.987
A safe agent may propose a ticket update, draft an email, or prepare a database change, but the execution path should remain explicit.

17
00:01:59.987 --> 00:02:09.589
This pattern keeps automation fast for low-risk work and deliberate for actions that could affect people, money, access, data, or operations.
